\pard\sb260\sa100\keepn\fs32\cf1\b B. Security Implementation Details\b0\cf0\fs20\par
\pard\sb260\sa100\keepn\fs32\cf1\b 1. Authentication and authorization 2\b0\cf0\fs20\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 Passwords are stored as bcrypt hashes using cost 12. Registration and login validate values on the server. Login uses a generic error for unknown email or incorrect password and is rate-limited. Successful registration and login regenerate the session ID. The auth middleware requires a signed-in session; the role middleware requires admin for admin endpoints. Student application queries are scoped to the session user.\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 Source references: server.js registration/login handlers, bcrypt.hash and bcrypt.compare, auth and role middleware, /api/applications, and /api/admin routes. Passwords are not stored or logged in plaintext.\par
\pard\sb100\sa120\brdrt\brdrl\brdrb\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf2\highlight2\cf1\fs19\b FIGURE 1 - Registration and sign-in pages\b0\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa70\highlight2\cf0\fs18 Capture both forms in a browser using test data. Keep the browser address bar visible and do not show real student details.\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa100\highlight2\cf1\fs17 Evidence status: Capture from the running application before final submission.\highlight0\par
\pard\sb100\sa120\brdrt\brdrl\brdrb\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf2\highlight2\cf1\fs19\b FIGURE 2 - bcrypt hashing and server-side RBAC\b0\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa70\highlight2\cf0\fs18 Capture bcrypt code, auth/role middleware, and a student request to an administrator endpoint returning 403.\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa100\highlight2\cf1\fs17 Evidence status: Capture from the running application before final submission.\highlight0\par
\pard\sb260\sa100\keepn\fs32\cf1\b 2. Data encryption and protection 2\b0\cf0\fs20\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 Names, emails, and student IDs are encrypted with AES-256-GCM using a random 12-byte IV and an authentication tag. The 32-byte key is read from DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY in .env. A keyed HMAC of normalized email supports lookup without a plaintext email index. Passwords use bcrypt. SQL values use bound parameters.\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 Selected identity fields are encrypted at rest; this does not encrypt the whole SQLite file, logs, backups, or host filesystem. Losing the key makes those encrypted values unrecoverable. Key rotation requires migration. Never include secret values in screenshots or a repository.\par
\pard\sb100\sa120\brdrt\brdrl\brdrb\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf2\highlight2\cf1\fs19\b FIGURE 3 - AES-GCM protection at rest\b0\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa70\highlight2\cf0\fs18 Capture encryption/decryption code and a database row showing ciphertext and a password hash. Redact the key and personal data.\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa100\highlight2\cf1\fs17 Evidence status: Capture from the running application before final submission.\highlight0\par
\pard\sb260\sa100\keepn\fs32\cf1\b 3. Input validation and output sanitization 2\b0\cf0\fs20\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 Zod schemas enforce types, formats, length bounds, numeric ranges, and allowed status values on the server. Request bodies are limited to 20 KB. Prepared SQL statements prevent input from changing query structure. Browser code escapes HTML-significant characters before inserting dynamic text. Helmet CSP limits script and style sources to the local origin and blocks object embedding.\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 HTML input constraints provide usability but are not the security boundary; server validation is. This version uses contextual HTML encoding in its render helper and does not include a separate DOMPurify package.\par
\pard\sb100\sa120\brdrt\brdrl\brdrb\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf2\highlight2\cf1\fs19\b FIGURE 4 - Validation, parameterized queries, CSP, and output encoding\b0\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa70\highlight2\cf0\fs18 Capture Zod schemas, a prepared query, the escape helper, and an invalid-input response with a safe message.\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa100\highlight2\cf1\fs17 Evidence status: Capture from the running application before final submission.\highlight0\par
\pard\sb260\sa100\keepn\fs32\cf1\b 4. Session management 2\b0\cf0\fs20\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 Sessions are stored in SQLite with a 30-minute rolling expiry. Login and registration regenerate the session identifier. Cookies use HttpOnly and SameSite=Strict. Secure is enabled when direct TLS is configured or NODE_ENV is production. State-changing routes require a random session-bound CSRF token. Logout destroys the server-side session and clears the cookie.\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 The current local environment uses HTTP, so it does not demonstrate the Secure cookie attribute. Capture cookie flags only after using HTTPS.\par
\pard\sb100\sa120\brdrt\brdrl\brdrb\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf2\highlight2\cf1\fs19\b FIGURE 5 - Session expiration, cookie flags, CSRF, and logout\b0\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa70\highlight2\cf0\fs18 Capture session configuration, the CSRF request, cookie flags on HTTPS, and a logged-out API request returning 401. Redact cookie values.\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa100\highlight2\cf1\fs17 Evidence status: Capture from the running application before final submission.\highlight0\par
\pard\sb260\sa100\keepn\fs32\cf1\b 5. Error handling and logging 2\b0\cf0\fs20\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 Asynchronous route errors go to a central handler that returns a generic message instead of stack traces or database details. Winston writes timestamped JSON events to logs/security.log and the console. Events cover registration, login success/failure, logout, application submission/review, and club changes. Passwords, CSRF tokens, and session IDs are not logged.\par
\pard\sb100\sa120\brdrt\brdrl\brdrb\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf2\highlight2\cf1\fs19\b FIGURE 6 - Safe error handling and Winston audit log\b0\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa70\highlight2\cf0\fs18 Capture the error handler, logger setup, a safe user-facing error, and a minimized structured log entry.\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa100\highlight2\cf1\fs17 Evidence status: Capture from the running application before final submission.\highlight0\par
\pard\sb260\sa100\keepn\fs32\cf1\b 6. Secure communication 2\b0\cf0\fs20\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 The app can serve HTTPS directly when TLS key and certificate paths are set. A trusted reverse proxy can terminate TLS; TRUST_PROXY=1 is appropriate only behind a trusted proxy. The current local run is HTTP at 127.0.0.1:3000. The application does not enforce an HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect itself, so secure transport is not yet demonstrated for this local run.\par
\pard\sb100\sa120\brdrt\brdrl\brdrb\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf2\highlight2\cf1\fs19\b FIGURE 7 - HTTPS and Secure cookie configuration\b0\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa70\highlight2\cf0\fs18 Capture only after a valid TLS certificate is configured: HTTPS address, certificate details, and Secure cookie attribute. Local HTTP is not HTTPS evidence.\par\pard\li180\ri180\sa100\highlight2\cf1\fs17 Evidence status: Capture from the running application before final submission.\highlight0\par
\pard\sb260\sa100\keepn\fs32\cf1\b 7. Security testing and auditing 2\b0\cf0\fs20\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 Evidence available: JavaScript syntax checks passed for server.js and public/app.js. The developer-provided console output shows /api/health returned HTTP 200 with ok=true. The PowerShell parsing warning was emitted by Invoke-WebRequest; it was followed by a successful 200 response. These checks do not verify login, authorization, injection handling, CSRF, rate limiting, or HTTPS.\par
\pard\sa100\sl260\slmult1\fs20\cf0 No OWASP ZAP scan or full penetration test is claimed. Complete Section G with actual results. GDPR, FERPA, and HIPAA compliance is not claimed; the institution must determine applicable rules and operational controls.\par
